Here we can see that for positive values of \(\lambda\), the function decays to zero as time increases, whereas for negative values of \(\lambda\), the function grows rapidly with time. However, as we will see later, the boundary conditions will restrict the possible values of \(\lambda\) to be non-negative, ensuring that \(T(t)\) always decays over time.

Infinite sum of eigenmodes
Substituting the space-dependent part of the solution \(X_n(x)Y_m(y)\) and the time-dependent part of the solution \(T(t)\) into the original assumption of separation of variables (shown in Equation 1.1), we have that each separated solution (or eigenmode) associated with the pair \((n,m)\) can be written as \[
\begin{aligned}
u_{n,m}(x,y,t) &= A_n B_m C_t \Phi_{n,m}(x,y)\, e^{-D \lambda_{n,m} t}
\end{aligned}
\]
where the overall multiplicative factor \(A_n B_m C_t\) can be absorbed into a single mode amplitude denoted by \(C_{n,m}\).
\[
u_{n,m}(x,y,t) = C_{n,m} \Phi_{n,m}(x,y)\, e^{-D \lambda_{n,m} t}
\]






NoteOrthogonality of cosine functions





Two functions \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\) are said to be orthogonal on an interval \([a,b]\) if the integral of their product over that interval is zero:
\[
\int_a^b f(x) g(x) \, dx = 0
\]
This can also be expressed in terms of the inner product of functions: \[
\langle f, g \rangle = 0
\] which implies that \(f\) and \(g\) are “perpendicular” to each other in the function space. If we imagine these functions as vectors, then orthogonality means that the two functions point in different directions and have no overlap.
Both \(\{\cos(\frac{n \pi x}{L})\}_{n=0}^{\infty}\) and \(\{\Phi_{n,m}(x,y)\}_{n,m=0}^{\infty}\) are sets of orthogonal functions.






NoteOrthogonality of \({\cos(\frac{n \pi x}{L})}\) in one dimension





For any fixed \(L &gt; 0\), the cosine functions \(\{\cos(\frac{n \pi x}{L})\}_{n=0}^{\infty}\) form an orthogonal basis on the interval \([0,L]\) for functions with zero derivative at the boundaries. This means that if we take any two different functions from this set, say \(\phi_n(x)\) and \(\phi_m(x)\): \[
\phi_{n}(x) = \cos\left(\frac{n \pi x}{L}\right) \quad n \in \mathbb{Z}^{\geq 0}
\tag{2.6}\]
\[
\phi_{m}(x) = \cos\left(\frac{m \pi x}{L}\right) \quad m \in \mathbb{Z}^{\geq 0}
\tag{2.7}\]
then the integral of their product is zero if \(n \neq m\). Mathematically, this orthogonality condition can be expressed as: \[
\int_0^L \phi_{n}(x) \phi_{m}(x) \, dx =
\begin{cases}
0 & \text{for all } n \neq m \\
\frac{L}{2} & \text{if } n=m \text{ and } n,m &gt; 0 \\
L & \text{if } n=m=0
\end{cases}
\tag{2.8}\]






NoteStep-by-step verification of the orthogonality property





To verify this Equation 2.8, we can use the trigonometric identity for the product of cosines: \[\cos A \cos B = \frac{1}{2} [\cos(A-B) + \cos(A+B)]\] Applying this identity to the integral gives: \[
\int_0^L \cos\left(\frac{n \pi x}{L}\right) \cos\left(\frac{m \pi x}{L}\right) dx
= \frac{1}{2} \int_0^L \left[ \cos\left(\frac{(n-m) \pi x}{L}\right) + \cos\left(\frac{(n+m) \pi x}{L}\right) \right] dx
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
=& \frac{1}{2} \left[ \int_0^L \cos\left(\frac{(n-m) \pi x}{L}\right) dx + \int_0^L \cos\left(\frac{(n+m) \pi x}{L}\right) dx \right] \\
=& \frac{1}{2} \left[ \frac{L}{(n-m)\pi} \sin\left(\frac{(n-m) \pi x}{L}\right) \Big|_0^L + \frac{L}{(n+m)\pi} \sin\left(\frac{(n+m) \pi x}{L}\right) \Big|_0^L \right] \\
  =& \frac{1}{2} \left[ \frac{L}{(n-m)\pi} \sin((n-m) \pi) + \frac{L}{(n+m)\pi} \sin((n+m) \pi) \right] =\\
\end{aligned}
\]

When \(n \neq m\), the above expression simplifies to zero since \(\sin(k \pi) = 0\) for any integer \(k\),
When \(n = m &gt; 0\), the integral becomes: \[
=\frac{1}{2} \lim_{\rho \to 0} \left[ \frac{L}{\rho \pi} \sin(\rho \pi) + \frac{L}{(2n)\pi} \sin(2n \pi) \right]
\]

Using the limit \(\lim_{\rho \to 0} \frac{\sin(\rho \pi)}{\rho \pi} = 1\), we get: \[
= \frac{1}{2} \left[ L + 0 \right] = \frac{L}{2}
\]

When \(n = m = 0\), the integral becomes: \[
\int_0^L \cos^2(0) dx = \int_0^L 1 dx = L
\]










