NoteOrthogonality of \(\Phi_{n,m}(x,y)\) in two dimensions





For any fixed \(L_x &gt; 0\) and \(L_y &gt; 0\), the set of functions \(\{\Phi_{n,m}(x,y)\}_{n,m \ge 0}\) form an orthogonal basis on the domain \(\Omega = [0,L_x]\times[0,L_y]\) for functions with zero derivative at the boundaries. This means that if we take any two different functions from this set, say \(\Phi_{n,m}(x,y)\) and \(\Phi_{p,q}(x,y)\), then the integral of their product over the domain is zero if \((n,m) \neq (p,q)\). Mathematically, this orthogonality condition can be expressed as: \[\int_{\Omega} \Phi_{n,m}(x,y) \Phi_{p,q}(x,y) \, dy \, dx =
\begin{cases}
0 & \text{for all } (n,m) \neq (p,q) \\
\frac{L_x L_y}{4} & \text{if } (n,m)=(p,q) \text{ and } n,m &gt; 0 \\
\frac{L_x L_y}{2} & \text{if } (n,m)=(p,q) \text{ and } (n=0 \text{ or } m=0) \\
L_x L_y & \text{if } (n,m)=(p,q)=(0,0)
\end{cases}
\tag{2.9}\]






NoteStep-by-step verification of the orthogonality property in two dimensions





To verify this Equation 2.9, we can use the fact that \(\Phi_{n,m}(x,y)\) is the product of two cosine functions, one in \(x\) and one in \(y\). This allows us to separate the double integral into the product of two single integrals, each of which can be evaluated using the orthogonality property of the cosine functions in one dimension.
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\int_0^{L_x} \int_0^{L_y} \Phi_{n,m}(x,y) \Phi_{p,q}(x,y) \, dy \, dx = \\
=& \int_0^{L_x} \int_0^{L_y} \cos\left(\frac{n \pi}{L_x} x\right) \cos\left(\frac{m \pi}{L_y} y\right) \cos\left(\frac{p \pi}{L_x} x\right) \cos\left(\frac{q \pi}{L_y} y\right) \, dy \, dx \\
=& \underbrace{\int_0^{L_x} \cos\left(\frac{n \pi}{L_x} x\right) \cos\left(\frac{p \pi}{L_x} x\right) dx}_{I_x(n,p)} \cdot \underbrace{\int_0^{L_y} \cos\left(\frac{m \pi}{L_y} y\right) \cos\left(\frac{q \pi}{L_y} y\right) dy}_{I_y(m,q)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Here we can use the results shown in Equation 2.8 for the integrals \(I_x(n,p)\) and \(I_y(m,q)\):
\[
I_{x}(n,p) =
\begin{cases}
\frac{L_x}{2} & \text{if } n=p \text{ and } n,p &gt; 0 \\
L_x & \text{if } n=p=0 \\
0 & \text{if } n \neq p
\end{cases}
\] \[
I_{y}(m,q) =
\begin{cases}
\frac{L_y}{2} & \text{if } m=q \text{ and } m,q &gt; 0 \\
L_y & \text{if } m=q=0 \\
0 & \text{if } m \neq q
\end{cases}
\]
Evaluating the product \(I_x(n,p) \cdot I_y(m,q)\) gives us the following cases:

When either \(n \neq p\) or \(m \neq q\), at least one of the integrals \(I_x\) or \(I_y\) will be zero, which makes the whole product zero.
When both \(n = p &gt; 0\) and \(m = q &gt; 0\), the integral will be \(\frac{L_x}{2} \cdot \frac{L_y}{2} = \frac{L_x L_y}{4}\).
When \(n = p = 0\) and \(m = q &gt; 0\), then \(I_x = L_x\) and \(I_y = \frac{L_y}{2}\), so the integral would be \(\frac{L_x L_y}{2}\).
When \(n = p &gt; 0\) and \(m = q = 0\), then \(I_x = \frac{L_x}{2}\) and \(I_y = L_y\), so the integral would also be \(\frac{L_x L_y}{2}\).
When \(n = p = 0\) and \(m = q = 0\), then \(I_x = L_x\) and \(I_y = L_y\), so the integral would be \(L_x \cdot L_y = L_x L_y\).










